    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Greek has the longest recorded history of any living Indo-European language, from the Mycenaean tablets of the 14th century BCE through the classical language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to the Greek spoken today.</li><li>It was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, the Athenian dramatists, historians and philosophers, and, after Alexander’s conquests, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine</a> or “common” Greek, the shared language of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</li><li>Its alphabet, the first to write vowels as well as consonants, became the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Cyrillic alphabets, and its words supply the terms of science, medicine, philosophy and politics in languages across the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The oldest Greek writing survived by accident: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets were records of unbaked clay, meant to be reused, until the fires that destroyed the palaces of Knossos and Pylos baked them hard.</p></aside>
    </section>

