    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-b"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linear-b.jpg" alt="Linear B" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Linear_B_tablet_of_Pylos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sharon Mollerus, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek is a member of the Indo-European family and the only surviving language of its Hellenic branch. Speakers of an early form of Greek probably entered the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> around the end of the 3rd millennium BCE, where they met earlier peoples whose languages left their mark in Greek: place names ending in -nthos and -ssos, such as Korinthos and Parnassos, and words such as thalassa, “sea”, and labyrinthos are thought to come from these lost languages.</p><p>The earliest written Greek is Mycenaean, recorded on clay tablets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> script, a syllabary adapted from the still undeciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> of Minoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. The tablets, found at Knossos on Crete, from around 1400 BCE, and at Pylos, Mycenae and Thebes on the mainland, from the 13th century BCE, are inventories of palace economies: lists of land, livestock, textiles, weapons and offerings to the gods. In 1952 the English architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a> showed that the language of Linear B was Greek, more than seven centuries older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>.</p><p>After the fall of the Mycenaean palaces around 1200 BCE, writing disappeared from Greece for some four centuries, except on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which kept a syllabic script of its own. Greek continued to be spoken, dividing into dialects: Ionic and Attic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the islands and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>; Aeolic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>; Arcadian and Cypriot; and Doric in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, Corinth, Crete and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>.</p><p>In the 8th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> adopted the alphabet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, merchants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, whose script wrote only consonants. The Greeks used Phoenician letters for consonant sounds that Greek lacked to write vowels, so that aleph became alpha and yod became iota, creating the first full alphabet. The earliest alphabetic inscriptions, such as a verse scratched on a wine jug from Athens and the “cup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a>” from the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a>, date from the second half of the 8th century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/homer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancient Greek was a highly inflected language. Its nouns had five cases, nominative, genitive, dative, accusative and vocative, three genders and, besides the singular and the plural, a dual for pairs, which faded during the classical period. Its verbs were among the richest of any Indo-European language, with forms for tense and aspect, four moods, including the optative for wishes and possibilities, and three voices, active, middle and passive, as well as a wealth of participles. A definite article, ho, hē, to, developed from a demonstrative; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> still uses it rarely.</p><p>Its sounds included vowels of two lengths, consonants with an audible breath, written ph, th and kh, which were pronounced like the p, t and k of English pin rather than the f and th of later Greek, and a musical pitch accent, in which a syllable was raised in tone rather than stressed. Scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, around 200 BCE, invented the written accents and breathings to help readers pronounce the old poetry correctly.</p><p>The alphabet varied from city to city until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> officially adopted the Ionic alphabet of twenty-four letters in 403 BCE, and other cities followed. Greek was written in capital letters only; the lowercase letters used today developed from a cursive book hand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in the 9th century CE.</p><p>The dialects of Greek were also literary languages: the epics were composed in a mixed language based on Ionic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> wrote in the Aeolic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, choral lyric used a form of Doric, and Attic, the dialect of Athens, became the language of tragedy, comedy, history, philosophy and oratory. Word order was free, and many small particles shaded the meaning of sentences, giving Greek prose its flexibility and subtlety.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 8th century BCE Greek settlers founded cities around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, from Marseille and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to Syracuse and Cyrene, carrying their language across the ancient world. In the 5th and 4th centuries BCE the cultural and political prestige of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> made its Attic dialect the most influential form of Greek.</p><p>The conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, between 334 and 323 BCE, transformed Greek into a world language. Under the Hellenistic kingdoms that followed, Greek was the language of government, trade and culture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, where a Greek inscription of maxims copied from Delphi was set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>. A common form of the language, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine</a>, based on Attic, replaced the old dialects and was spoken as a first or second language by millions of people of many origins.</p><p>Under Roman rule, Greek remained the language of the eastern half of the empire and of philosophy, medicine and science throughout it; educated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> learned Greek, and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> wrote his Meditations in it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, the Greek translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> scriptures, and the books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> were written in Koine, which became the first language of the Christian church.</p><p>The Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, founded in 330, was a Greek-speaking state, which replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> with Greek as its language of government in the 7th century. Around this time, scholars conventionally mark the end of ancient Greek and the beginning of Medieval Greek, the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for nearly a thousand years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iliad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iliad.jpg" alt="Iliad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illiad_fragmented_papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Homer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek literature begins with two of its greatest works, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and composed around 700 BCE from a long tradition of oral poetry. It continued with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>’s poems on the gods and farming, and with the lyric poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>.</p><p>The 5th and 4th centuries BCE, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, produced the tragedies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and the comedies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>; history, invented as a form of inquiry by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and refined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>; the speeches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>; and philosophy, from the dialogues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> to the treatises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, which covered almost every field of knowledge.</p><p>In the Hellenistic and Roman periods, Greek was the language of science and scholarship: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>’s Elements of geometry, the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>, the medical writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a>’ school and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, the astronomy of Ptolemy, and the critical scholarship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>, which edited and preserved the classics. Biographers, novelists, satirists and historians, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Lucian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, continued the tradition, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and the works of the Church Fathers made Greek the language of Christian thought.</p><p>Much of this literature survives because Byzantine scholars copied it for centuries; papyri found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> since the 19th century, above all at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a>, have restored works thought lost, such as plays of Menander and poems of Sappho.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras.jpg" alt="Manuel Chrysoloras" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras">Manuel Chrysoloras</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel-chrysoloras-paolo-uccello-louvre-15th.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paolo Uccello., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek changed gradually from the ancient to the modern language. In the centuries around the beginning of the Common Era, the distinction between long and short vowels was lost, the pitch accent became a stress accent, and many vowels and diphthongs merged into the sound i. The dative case, the optative and the infinitive gradually disappeared, and the future came to be formed with a particle, as in Modern Greek.</p><p>Educated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> reacted against this change. From the 1st century BCE, writers imitated the Attic of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, opening a long gap between the written and the spoken language. This gap lasted into modern times: after independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> used a purified literary form, Katharevousa, for government and education, until in 1976 the popular language, Demotic, was made the official language; the old system of accents was simplified in 1982.</p><p>In western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, knowledge of Greek almost vanished in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its revival began when the Byzantine scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras">Manuel Chrysoloras</a> began teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1397; Greek scholars brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before and after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453; the printer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> published the Greek classics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> published the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in 1516, an event of great importance for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><p>Modern Greek, spoken by about 13 million people in Greece, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the diaspora, is the descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine</a>. A few dialects preserve older features: Tsakonian, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, descends in part from ancient Doric, and Greek dialects survive in southern Italy and among the Pontic Greeks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Greek alphabet is one of the most influential inventions in history. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> it gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, now the most widely used in the world; around 900 the Cyrillic alphabet was created for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, largely from Greek letters; and it inspired the Coptic, Gothic and Armenian scripts. The word alphabet itself comes from its first two letters, alpha and beta.</p><p>Greek vocabulary pervades the languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and beyond. Words such as democracy, politics, philosophy, history, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, music, mathematics, physics and geography are Greek, and scientists continue to build new terms from Greek roots, from telephone and photography to the names of diseases, elements and species.</p><p>Greek literature and thought form one of the foundations of Western culture, and their influence reached the Islamic world, whose scholars translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> into Arabic in the 8th to 10th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and the theology of the early Church were written in Greek, which remains the language of the liturgy of the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><p>For centuries, the study of ancient Greek was at the heart of education in Europe, and it is still taught in schools and universities around the world, keeping open a direct line to the words of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and Aristotle.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ancient Greek, the Indo-European language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, written from the Mycenaean tablets of the 14th century BCE, was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, Athenian drama, history and philosophy, and, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine</a> after Alexander, of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. Its alphabet became the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Cyrillic, and it lives on in Modern Greek and in the vocabulary of science.</em></p>

